<div class="postcard-grid" style="max-width: 700px; margin: 2rem auto 0;">
  <!-- Entry 1 -->
  <div class="postcard-card">
    <div class="back">
      <div class="back-content">
        <h2>RTX 5060 Ti — 9GB Coming</h2>
        <p>First-gen 5060 Ti launched with 8GB and got roasted by everyone. NVIDIA is patching that with <strong>9GB GDDR7 variants</strong> launching <strong>May–June 2026</strong>, around Computex time.</p>
        <p>Reports suggest the 9GB bump comes from memory density changes rather than a wider bus — so it's a fix, not a redesign. Still, it's better than the 8GB card that was already competitive on paper.</p>
        <p><em>Watch for: official spec confirmation around May 2026. The 5060 family was announced but exact SKUs are still rolling out.</em></p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="front">
      <div class="front-content">
        <span class="badge badge-news">news</span>
        <h3>RTX 5060 Ti Gets 9GB</h3>
        <p>The fix NVIDIA didn't know it needed.</p>
        <p class="front-meta">May–Jun 2026</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- Entry 2 -->
  <div class="postcard-card">
    <div class="back">
      <div class="back-content">
        <h2>SUPER Refresh — Cancelled</h2>
        <p>RTX 5080 Ti SUPER and RTX 5070 Ti SUPER both <strong>cancelled</strong>. There's no mid-cycle refresh in 2026.</p>
        <p>This means the Blackwell lineup is effectively frozen for the year. 2026 is officially a <strong>holding pattern</strong> — no new architecture, no refresh, just the cards we got in early 2025.</p>
        <p><em>This is actually good for 3090 sellers: if nothing new hits the mid-range, the 3090's value proposition stays stronger. The 5070 Ti at $549 launch price is the "strong value" card, but it doesn't compete with 24GB 3090s for workloads that need VRAM.</em></p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="front">
      <div class="front-content">
        <span class="badge badge-rumor">analysis</span>
        <h2>2026: A Holding Pattern Year</h2>
        <p>No SUPER refresh. No new architecture. Just the cards we got.</p>
        <p class="front-meta">Status: frozen</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- Entry 3 -->
  <div class="postcard-card">
    <div class="back">
      <div class="back-content">
        <h2>5090 AIB Prices Gone Wild</h2>
        <p>MSRP is $1,999. Custom AIB models start around <strong>$2,900</strong>, climb past <strong>$3,400</strong>, and liquid-cooled variants are pushing <strong>past $5,000</strong>.</p>
        <p>The Founders Edition stays closest to MSRP. The premium cards are... something else entirely. This is a market where people are spending more for a GPU than most people spent on their entire first car.</p>
        <p><em>Meanwhile the 3090 sits at C$1,000–1,200 used. Sometimes the old guard still throws a punch.</em></p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="front">
      <div class="front-content">
        <span class="badge badge-market">market</span>
        <h3>5090 Prices Hit $5,000</h3>
        <p>Custom cards. Liquid-cooled. $5K. Why not.</p>
        <p class="front-meta">AIB pricing</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- Entry 4 -->
  <div class="postcard-card">
    <div class="back">
      <div class="back-content">
        <h2>PRAGMATA Bundle</h2>
        <p>NVIDIA is bundling a free copy of <strong>PRAGMATA</strong> (Standard Edition on Steam) with RTX 5070+ purchases. Bundle runs <strong>April 14 – May 12, 2026</strong>, with redemption until <strong>June 9</strong>.</p>
        <p>Applies to select RTX 5090, 5080, 5070 Ti, and 5070 cards. RTX 5060 series is excluded. "While supplies last" — classic NVIDIA.</p>
        <p><em>PRAGMATA is the highly anticipated game from Kazuma Kujo (creator of Onimusha). Whether it's actually good is a separate question, but free is free.</em></p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="front">
      <div class="front-content">
        <span class="badge badge-news">news</span>
        <h3>Free PRAGMATA with RTX 5070+</h3>
        <p>Free game. Expired in a month. Classic.</p>
        <p class="front-meta">Through May 12</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- Entry 5 -->
  <div class="postcard-card">
    <div class="back">
      <div class="back-content">
        <h2>16GB VRAM Supply Shortage</h2>
        <p>NVIDIA reportedly <strong>cutting production of 16GB VRAM cards</strong> due to an ongoing memory supply shortage that started at the end of 2025.</p>
        <p>This could tighten mid-range supply further and affect cards like the 5070 Ti (16GB) and any future 5060 Ti SKUs that exceed 9GB. It also indirectly supports the 3090's 24GB advantage — if NVIDIA can't supply 16GB cards, the 3090's larger VRAM pool becomes even more valuable for AI/ML workloads.</p>
        <p><em>For Moss's 3090 sale specifically: a tightening 16GB supply could mean the 3090's 24GB becomes a stronger differentiator in the used market.</em></p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="front">
      <div class="front-content">
        <span class="badge badge-market">market</span>
        <h3>16GB VRAM Supply Tightening</h3>
        <p>The shortage that's reshaping the mid-range.</p>
        <p class="front-meta">Supply chain</p>
      </div>
    </div>
